(Abstract) Tj
T*
(We performed secondary analyses on 183 patients from our prevention trial, focusing on the) Tj
T*
(predictive accuracy of standardized risk assessment at different care transitions and the) Tj
T*
(relationship between documented preventive actions and clinical outcomes. Formal risk) Tj
T*
(assessment was documented in only 34% of prehospital encounters, 56% of emergency) Tj
T*
(department admissions, and 87% of ward admissions. Among 75 patients stratified as high-) Tj
T*
(risk at ward admission, 41 \(55%\) subsequently developed tissue breakdown, whereas among) Tj
T*
(108 low-risk patients, 15 \(14%\) developed injury, giving a 3.9 relative risk. Notably,) Tj
T*
(tissue breakdown did not occur exclusively in high-risk patients; 23% of low-risk) Tj
T*
(stratified patients experienced injury, suggesting assessment limitations. Documented) Tj
T*
(preventive measures \(positioning records, turning schedules\) were more complete at the) Tj
T*
(ward level but adherence to these protocols was inconsistent. Patients who received) Tj
T*
(preventive interventions within the first 8 hours of admission had lower injury rates \(16%) Tj
T*
(vs 28%\). The protective device was associated with 35% relative risk reduction in the) Tj
T*
(subgroup receiving it within 6 hours of presentation. Cost-effectiveness analysis) Tj
T*
(suggested that early systematic assessment with targeted intervention would be cost-) Tj
T*
(neutral compared to treating established injuries.) Tj
